Does LLM Pre-Training Typically Occur at the Edge of Stability? Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.36778v1 [cs.LG] 27 Oct 2026 Does LLM Pre-Training Typically Occur at the Edge of Stability? Yuhang Cai *† Haodong Wen *‡ willcai@berkeley.edu whd25@mails.tsinghua.edu.cn Haofeng Huang *‡ Deyi Liu § huanghf22@mails.tsinghua.edu.cn deyiliu@bytedance.com Yiyuan Ma § Kaifeng Lyu ‡ mayiyuan.unicorn@bytedance.com klyu@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn Abstract Quadratic approximations provide a common lens for studying neural network optimization, but recent evidence challenges their predictive validity. In full-batch gradient descent with learning rate (LR) η \eta , Cohen et al. (2021) observed the Edge of Stability (EoS), where the largest Hessian eigenvalue concentrates near 2 / η 2/\eta , seemingly at odds with classical stability conditions predicted by quadratic approximations. In this work, we revisit the fidelity of quadratic approximations as a model of neural network training dynamics, with a particular focus on their failure modes in LLM training. We first highlight a distinct failure mode of the quadratic approximation, where persistent negative curvature causes the quadratic dynamics to diverge regardless of the LR, even though the real dynamics remain stable. We term this phenomenon the Edge of Convexity (EoC). After removing the effect of this LR-independent instability, we then propose a generalized definition of Edge of Stability (EoS) for stochastic training with adaptive optimizers, based on whether increasing the LR induces significant divergence in the quadratic dynamics while leaving the real dynamics largely unchanged. Across different LLM pretraining runs with various model sizes up to 787 787 M, we find that (1) EoC is always observed across LLM pretraining, and (2) EoS is also prevalent but not universal: it disappears when the LR becomes sufficiently small (e.g., after decay) or when the batch size falls below a critical threshold that has a positive correlation with the critical batch size. Together, these findings characterize when and how quadratic approximations fail and provide a foundation for future work on understanding training dynamics. 1 1 footnotetext: Equal contribution. 2 2 footnotetext: Affiliations: † UC Berkeley; ‡ Tsinghua University; § ByteDance Seed. 1 Introduction In optimization theory, the dynamics of gradient-based methods are commonly analyzed through a local quadratic approximation of the loss function, given by the second-order Taylor expansion around the current iterate: ℒ ( 𝒘 t + 1 ) ≈ ℒ ( 𝒘 t ) + ( 𝒘 t + 1 − 𝒘 t ) ⊤ ∇ ℒ ( 𝒘 t ) + 1 2 ( 𝒘 t + 1 − 𝒘 t ) ⊤ ∇ 2 ℒ ( 𝒘 t ) ( 𝒘 t + 1 − 𝒘 t ) . \mathcal{L}( m{w}_{t+1})\approx\mathcal{L}( m{w}_{t})+( m{w}_{t+1}- m{w}_{t})^{	op}
abla\mathcal{L}( m{w}_{t})+ rac{1}{2}( m{w}_{t+1}- m{w}_{t})^{	op}
abla^{2}\mathcal{L}( m{w}_{t})( m{w}_{t+1}- m{w}_{t}). (1) This approximation yields the well-known descent lemma for gradient descent, which guarantees a decrease in loss when the LR η \eta satisfies η < 2 / λ max \eta<2/\lambda_{\max} . Here, λ max \lambda_{\max} is the maximum eigenvalue of the Hessian, which serves as a measure of the local curvature of the loss function. A standard analysis pipeline in optimization theory then proceeds by bounding quantities that appear in the lemma and deriving guarantees on the loss convergence. However, in deep learning, the quadratic approximation often fails to accurately capture the true training dynamics. Notably, Cohen et al. (2021) show that full-batch gradient descent (GD) typically operates at the Edge of Stability (EoS) when training neural networks. For a fixed LR η \eta , the maximum Hessian eigenvalue λ max \lambda_{\max} tends to increase during training and eventually oscillates around 2 / η 2/\eta , thus η ≈ 2 / λ max \eta\approx 2/\lambda_{\max} . Under the quadratic approximation, this places the dynamics exactly at the stability boundary of gradient descent, since the quadratic model predicts loss divergence once η > 2 / λ max \eta>2/\lambda_{\max} . Consequently, the descent lemma can no longer guarantee a persistent decrease of the loss. Yet in practice, the loss continues to decrease over long time scales. Similar EoS behavior has also been observed for full-batch adaptive optimizers such as Adam and RMSProp ( Cohen et al., 2022 ) , when curvature is measured using an appropriately preconditioned Hessian. (a) Illustration of Edge of Convexity (EoC). (b) Illustration of Edge of Stability (EoS). Figure 1: LLM pretraining on FineWeb-10B exhibits two distinct failure modes of the quadratic approximation. (a) EoC phenomenon : regardless of the LR choice, the training dynamics under quadratic approximation diverge due to persistent negative curvature, while the true dynamics remain stable. (b) EoS phenomenon : after compensating for negative curvature, the quadratic loss remains as stable as the true loss for 0.8 × 0.8	imes and 1.0 × 1.0	imes the training LR, but increasing the LR slightly to 1.2 × 1.2	imes causes the quadratic loss to diverge. This shows that the current training LR is close to a critical stability threshold. See Sections 5.1 and 5.2 for details. The EoS phenomenon therefore exposes a fundamental limitation of existing optimization analyses for deep learning, as many such analyses critically rely on the descent lemma and the underlying quadratic approximation. This discrepancy has motivated a line of work that seeks to develop tools to understand training dynamics without critically relying on the quadratic approximation ( Arora et al., 2022 ; Damian et al., 2022 ; Cohen et al., 2024 ) . Despite these advances, existing empirical observations and theoretical analyses remain limited in several important aspects: (i) Most theoretical definitions of EoS focus on full-batch training, whereas stochastic training is more common in practice; (ii) Most studies focus on failures of the quadratic approximation caused by large positive Hessian eigenvalues, even though negative Hessian eigenvalues can also induce a similar gap between theory and practice, as we will elaborate in this work; (iii) Due to the prohibitive computational cost of computing and analyzing the Hessian, direct measurements of EoS are largely limited to relatively small-scale settings such as CIFAR-10 and ImageNet, leaving open whether the same failure mode persists in large-scale training. Our Contributions. We present a systematic study of the failure modes of the quadratic approximation in stochastic, adaptive, and large-scale training, with a particular focus on LLM pretraining. • We first highlight an LR-independent failure mode of the quadratic approximation, where persistent negative curvature during training causes the quadratic dynamics to diverge regardless of the LR, even though the real dynamics remain stable. We term this phenomenon the Edge of Convexity (EoC). See Section 5.1 . • We isolate and remove the effect of EoC, and then study the Edge of Stability (EoS) as an LR-dependent failure mode. We propose a generalized definition of EoS for stochastic training with adaptive optimizers, based on whether increasing the LR induces significant divergence in the quadratic dynamics while leaving the real dynamics largely unchanged. For full-batch GD, our definition recovers the original EoS definition. See Sections 5.2 and 5.3 . • We design efficient methods to detect both EoC and EoS at scale with theoretical guarantees ( Sections A.2 and A.3 ). Our results on LLM pretraining suggest that (1) EoC consistently arises; (2) EoS is prevalent but not universal, and disappears when the LR becomes sufficiently small (e.g., after decay) or when the batch size falls below a critical threshold that scales linearly with the critical batch size ( Sections 6.2 , 6.3 and 6.4 ). (a) GPT-small (104M). (b) GPT-medium (344M). (c) GPT-large (787M). Figure 2: EoS and EoC appear across model sizes. At most checkpoints, the bottom heatmaps show a sharp transition from bounded displacement to explosion near LR factor 1.0 1.0 , indicating that the training LR is at the critical threshold of EoS. In the middle row, − λ min -\lambda_{\min} has a smaller magnitude than λ max \lambda_{\max} , corresponding to saddleness below the 0.1 0.1 reference line in the top plots and consistent with EoC. Each heatmap column is a checkpoint, and the vertical axis gives the simulated LR divided by the training LR. See Section 6.2 for the results and Section B.1 for training and detection settings. 2 Related Work Edge of Stability. Early studies connected sharpness and stability to optimizer behavior in neural network training: Wu et al. (2018) analyzed SGD minima selection through dynamical stability using sharpness and non-uniformity, and Xing et al. (2018) observed SGD “bouncing” between valley walls during training. Jastrzębski et al. (2018) then showed that SGD visits increasingly sharp regions up to a maximum set by LR and batch size, and that the step is often too large along the sharpest directions. Cohen et al. (2021) later demonstrated that full-batch gradient descent typically operates at an Edge of Stability (EoS), where the largest Hessian eigenvalue oscillates near 2 / η 2/\eta while loss decreases non-monotonically. Cohen et al. (2022) extended this phenomenon to adaptive methods via saturation of a preconditioned stability threshold. Finally, Arora et al. (2022) ; Li et al. (2022) ; Damian et al. (2022) ; Lyu et al. (2022) provide theoretical explanations for EoS in different training regimes, but a unified understanding of this phenomenon is still lacking. Training Stability of Neural Networks Series works studied the training stability of constant LR SGD in quadratic losses via a linear system analysis of the second moment of the parameters ( Wu et al., 2018 ; Ma & Ying, 2021 ; Granziol et al., 2022 ; Wu et al., 2022 ; Mulayoff & Michaeli, 2024 ) . They obtained tight conditions in the form of ‖ 𝔼 ⁡ [ ( 𝑰 − η ​ 𝑯 t ) ⊗ 2 ] ‖ ≤ 1 \|\mathbb{E}[({ m{I}}-\eta{ m{H}}_{t})^{\otimes 2}]\|\leq 1 , which are valid instability criteria for a particular Lyapunov function. However, in the training neural networks with (S)GD, the unstable convergence phenomenon was observed ( Wu et al., 2018 ; Xing et al., 2018 ; Jastrzębski et al., 2018 ) , formally identified and termed Edge of Stability (EoS) ( Cohen et al., 2021 ) . Subsequently, several studies sought to explain this unstable convergence ( Ma et al., 2022 ; Ahn et al., 2022 ; Wu et al., 2023 ; Arora et al., 2022 ; Damian et al., 2022 ; Cohen et al., 2024 ) . Among them, Cohen et al. (2024) gave a general characterization of the oscillatory dynamics of deep learning in the EoS regime via a time-averaged differential equation termed central flow . However, the effectiveness of the quadratic approximation was never carefully studied in the large-scale neural network training, hence it is not clear when and how the quadratic approximation fails or not in the realistic regime. Hessian Spectrum of Neural Networks. Most studies on the Hessian spectrum of neural networks reported that the Hessian spectra of neural networks consist of a “bulk” together with a few large “outliers” in magnitude ( LeCun et al., 2002 ; Dauphin et al., 2014 ; Sagun et al., 2016 ; Sagun et al., 2017 ; Ghorbani et al., 2019 ; Chaudhari et al., 2019 ; Yao et al., 2020 ) . Among them, Sagun et al. (2016) ; Sagun et al. (2017) mentioned that there are still negative eigenvalues during training, but their magnitude is much smaller than the large outliers. More recent works reported and explained a near-block diagonal structure in the Hessian spectrum of CNNs and Transformers ( Zhang et al., 2024 ; Dong et al., 2025 ) . However, one thing that has long been overlooked is the characterization of the negative eigenvalues in the population Hessian during large-scale training. In this work, we present detailed empirical findings of the negative eigenvalues across different setups in large-scale neural network training. Comparison with Andreyev & Beneventano (2024) . The recent work ( Andreyev & Beneventano, 2024 ) is among the most relevant to this work, which defines Edge of Stochastic Stability for SGD via the batch sharpness, where Batch Sharpness hovers around 2 / η 2/\eta . Compared to Andreyev & Beneventano (2024) , our work (1) In the full-batch setting, our definition for EoS can be reduced to the original definition of EoS by Cohen et al. (2021) ; Cohen et al. (2022) , while theirs cannot; (2) We also identify another failure mode of the quadratic approximation called Edge of Convexity (EoC); (3) We provide scalable EoS and EoC detection methods with theoretical guarantees; (4) We conduct experiments on large-scale LLM pretraining with AdamW, while most of their experiments are li